      "**so that + clause**: use it when the purpose has its own subject, usually with *can* or *will* (or *could* and *would* for the past): *I'll speak slowly so that everyone can understand. She whispered so that the baby wouldn't wake up.* In conversation, *that* is often dropped: *Turn it up so I can hear.*",
      "**so + adjective or adverb + that** shows a result: *The test was so hard that nobody finished. He spoke so fast that I couldn't follow.* Use *so many, so much, so few, so little* before a noun: *There were so many people that we couldn't get in.*",
      "**such (a) + (adjective) + noun + that**: *It was such a hot day that we went to the beach.* With plural and uncountable nouns, there's no *a*: *such nice people, such good weather*.",
      "**too… to or so… that?** *Too* + adjective + *to* means there's a problem, so you can't: *The box was too heavy to lift* = *The box was so heavy that I couldn't lift it.* Don't mix them: ✗ *too heavy that*.",
